{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cancellation law in an integral domain cancellation law in an integral domain","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain.md","fileName":"Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 00:29\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Integral Domains and Ring Characteristic]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Integral Domains and Ring Characteristic","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cancellation law in an integral domain cancellation law in an integral domain","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain.md","fileName":"Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain","heading":"Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"In an integral domain, ac = bc with nonzero c implies a = b. Subtracting gives (a-b)c = 0, and the absence of zero divisors forces a-b = 0.\n\nCancellation fails in general rings when the cancelled factor is a zero divisor.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Integral Domains and Ring Characteristic","essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cancellation Law in an Integral Domain.md","sourceType":"m